The brief was explicit: "Do not assume features exist because documentation
says they exist. Verify everything from the actual implementation," and "Do NOT
mark something A+ because it is 'good enough.'"

So nothing in this audit is graded from a comment or a page's own prose. Two
harnesses build synthetic rooms whose floor plane and object boxes are known by
construction, run them through the production analyseRoom() and
compositeFloor(), and count what actually happened. They live in scripts/audit,
they are NOT guards, they do not run in verify-all and they cannot fail a
build — they exist so anyone can re-execute a claim on any checkout.

THE GRADE IS B−, AND THREE FINDINGS CARRY IT.

AV-01 · the renderer paints hardwood over people. render.ts:474 is the entire
object-protection system: a pixel survives if and only if its CHROMA differs
from the floor's mean chroma by more than 0.075. No luminance, no texture, no
edge, no spatial coherence, no segmentation. So it is conservative against
colour, which makes it anti-conservative against exactly what stands on a
hardwood floor in a hardwood customer's house. Fourteen objects, six painted
over at 100% of their pixels: a jute rug, a human leg, a golden retriever, an
oak stair riser, a wooden table leg, a cardboard box. The eight survivors are
the ones whose hue is not wood. The renderer's own docblock promises the
opposite ("painting oak across the cat looks like a toy") and the code does not
meet it. Worse, the "a lot of this room is furniture" warning keys off
`painted`, which stays high precisely because the furniture was painted.

AV-02 · "measured" is asserted where it is not true. room.ts argues, correctly,
that a confidence percentage would be a claim about a distribution nobody
estimated. The argument is right; the boundary is wrong. Six rooms with a true
floor coverage of 0.55: empty room 0%, dark floor 0%, sofa 0%, angled 24% — and
a room with a rug 72% error, still labelled `measured`. A rug collapses the
colour run the way a wall does. `weak` fires only on degeneracies, never on the
most common photograph a homeowner will upload. The screen headed "We found
your room" says measured to someone whose floor has been cut to a sixth.

GC-026 · the studio prices a New York room in Canadian dollars. GEO-004 closed
GC-024 by establishing that a New York surface shows no Canadian figure.
verify-geo holds that on pages that name a market; the studio names none, so
the rule never reached it. bandForWork(work) sits at its 'CA' default,
Intl.NumberFormat is hard-coded to CAD, the WebApplication JSON-LD tells
machines the range is "in Canadian dollars", and the header and footer link
/floor-studio from all 26 US markets. A homeowner in Amherst reads USD on their
city page and is quoted CAD one click later. bandForWork(work, country) already
takes the argument; nothing calls it with one.

WHAT IS GENUINELY EXCELLENT IS RECORDED TOO, because an audit that only lists
faults is not an audit: the refusal to estimate area from an uncalibrated photo
(asked for instead, with the reason beside the field), the refusal to generate
floors that cannot be bought, the absence of any upload endpoint at all, the
single price source, the exact homography rather than an affine sticker, and
every disabled control explaining itself from chemistry rather than
merchandising.

Live camera, semantic and instance segmentation, monocular depth and surface
normals are MISSING against the brief — and the site never claims them, which
is a gap rather than a lie. "Use my camera" is capture="environment" on a file
input: real on a phone, a silent file picker on desktop.

No application source is touched by this patch. The fix queue it opens is
VIS-02 (AV-01), VIS-03 (AV-02), GEO-006 (GC-026), one patch each, each making
the system able to support a claim it is already making.

